                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "A game with infinite potential, the \"friendslop\" to conquer them all, even before the term was so widely spread (or even coined), turned out to be a scam from Cryptic Sea to its publisher Devolver Digital and its many fans..\nLike every multiplayer game, Sub Rosa had its array of sketchy individuals that would cause trouble outside and inside the game. The developer has seen this as an excuse to punish every player, fan or not, with the end of development, for doing things such as:\nNot buying his other game (a car game nothing like Sub Rosa)\nNot pamper criticism\nRequest content\nOffer help in the development\n\nSub Rosa is very dear to me, it holds memories of eternal joy, much of them with people that aren't in my life anymore, so it remains downloaded in my main storage device like a dusty relic, like a digital grasp on the very concept of nostalgia.\n It's release date was on 2021, but it still feels like a game that could very well have been developed in the early 2000s, when the internet wasn't the tool of control that it is today.\nThe mechanics are unique, not just the core gameplay, which is a kiss in the forehead for fans of movies such as Pulp Fiction, Reservoir Dogs, Fargo TV Series or No Country For Old Men; but also the feeling of guns, inventory, movement, vehicular handling, objectives and teamwork/communication.\n\nDo I recommend this game? To be blunt, no. It's today's answer (hoping to regret it). If I have been asked some years ago, even when the game was still abandoned, I would have said yes, for the sake of nostalgia. The love that has made this game has been buried in the concrete of resentment from its developer. I'm against publishers and other kind of enterprises grabbing the IP's of developers, like any artist whose creation has been stolen, but this is one of those exceptions, I hope that if the developer doesn't see a new light towards this creation, Devolver can use any legal structure to take it from him and continue the creation, or at least punish the developer tenfold for all the experiences that could have been.",
